  "query_preview": "..A Pacific Northwest storm system is making its way\ninto Montana this evening, with its associated cold front near a\nKENV-KEMM line. The frontal passage has been mostly dry so far,\nwith just a few very isolated cells developing in the vicinity of\nthe boundary. The front will continue slowly southeast overnight\nbefore eventually stalling and slowly weakening. The northwestern\nhalf of so of the forecast area will see significantly cooler\ntemperatures by tomorrow afternoon, up to 20F below today's highs,\nand around 10F below seasonal normals. \n\nAcross southeastern Utah, earlier showers and thunderstorms have\ndiminished, and the front is expected to slide the axis of deep\nmoisture a bit to the east, resulting in a bit more limited threat\nof convection tomorrow afternoon. For southern Utah, little change\nin temperatures is expected ahead of the front, with highs staying\nnear normal for this time of year. \n\nFor midweek and beyond, the details of the forecast look a bit\nmurkier than usual, with ensembles showing a good amount of\ndisagreement with regard to the track of the next Pacific\nNorthwest trough, particularly with how far south it might end up\nlater in the week. This would have implications for the amount of\nmoisture in place for western Utah, primarily. For eastern Utah,\nthe deep moisture never goes away, with the axis most likely\nshifting a bit westward, bringing increasing coverage of afternoon\nconvection.",
